  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "How does the screw make lifting easier?",
      "a": "The screw thread is a ramp wrapped round a rod. Each full turn moves the nut only one thread spacing, called the pitch, while your hand travels much further, so a small force becomes a large one.",
      "asks": [
        "Why does a screw multiply force?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What do the scissor arms do?",
      "a": "The arms form a diamond. When the screw pulls the side corners together, the diamond gets taller and pushes the car up.",
      "asks": [
        "How does the diamond shape lift?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why does a jack not drop back down?",
      "a": "The thread's slope is so shallow that friction stops the car's weight from turning the screw backwards. It only moves when you turn the handle.",
      "asks": [
        "Why does the car stay up?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why do I turn the handle so many times?",
      "a": "Energy in equals energy out. To lift a heavy car a few centimetres with a small force, your hand must travel a long way, which means many turns.",
      "asks": [
        "Why is jacking a car slow?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Is a jack safe to work under?",
      "a": "No. A jack is only for lifting. Before anyone goes under a car, it should rest on proper axle stands.",
      "asks": [
        "Can I go under a car on a jack?"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "facts": [
    "The pitch of a screw is the distance it moves in one full turn.",
    "A screw thread is an inclined plane wrapped round a cylinder."
  ],
